LD2R/LD3R/LD4R -- load one element per register and REPLICATE it.

A different family from the multiple-structures forms 0072-0074 restored: those
distribute one contiguous block across N registers, while LD<n>R reads N
consecutive elements and splats each across its own destination.

All three had `return false` STUBS in Decode.cpp and no semantics, so they were
fatal `undecoded instruction` -- the loud failure mode, not the silent one.
Only `LD1R_ASISDLSO_R1` was ever implemented.

Where it bites: Go reaches LD4R during runtime start-up, in
`internal/chacha8rand.block` (the runtime PRNG):

    1ef44: 4d60e940  ld4r {v0.4s-v3.4s}, [x10]

Measured 2026-09-01: with 0075 applied a Go 1.26 hello-world LIFTS but stops
here at run time. ⚠️ `gosu` (Go 1.24) also contains four `ld[1234]r` and is
unaffected only because it dies in `mallocinit` before reaching them -- so this
is not a Go-version difference either, just a difference in how far each binary
gets.

The decoder mirrors `TryDecodeLD1R_ASISDLSO_R1` with N destinations, wrapping
the register number at 32 as `TryDecodeLDn` does. ❗ The memory operand keeps the
SINGLE-element size, exactly as LD1R's does: the semantics walks to the 2nd..Nth
elements with `GetElementPtr`, so the operand must stay the one-element type
(`M32` for `.4s`) or it would not match the semantic's parameter.

The multi-value returns reuse `TPair` and the `TTriple`/`TQuad` added in 0073.
No framework support is needed -- the lifter writes an N-element struct return
across N destination registers generically.

Not covered: the post-index replicate forms (`*_ASISDLSOP_R*_I` and
`*_ASISDLSOP_RX*_R`). They keep their stubs and so still fail loudly, and
nothing measured in any fixture executes one.

